@node Overview
@chapter Overview

niori-pdf generates PDF 1.4 files entirely inside GNU Emacs.  It is a
port of the core of Marc Battyani's Common Lisp library @uref{
https://github.com/mbattyani/cl-pdf, cl-pdf}, plus a practical subset of
its layout engine cl-typesetting, adapted to idiomatic Emacs Lisp.

The reason for the port: org-export and @code{latex} make Emacs depend
on a TeX installation.  A standalone Emacs application (such as
niori.el) cannot require TeX, Ghostscript or a headless browser on the
user's machine.  niori-pdf writes standard PDF files with nothing but a
running Emacs; its only dependency is Emacs 27.1 or newer.

The package is split into modules, each corresponding to a layer of the
original library:

@itemize
@item @file{niori-pdf-core.el} --- object model, file writer, xref,
compression (@pxref{Compression})
@item @file{niori-pdf-metrics.el} --- generated AFM width tables for the
base-14 fonts
@item @file{niori-pdf-fonts.el} --- font registration, WinAnsi encoding,
text measurement
@item @file{niori-pdf-ttf.el} --- TrueType/OpenType parsing, subsetting,
embedding (including TrueType collections)
@item @file{niori-pdf-type1.el} --- Type 1 font (.pfb/.pfa) parsing and
embedding
@item @file{niori-pdf-content.el} --- text and vector graphics operators
(the pdf-base/text/pdf-geom equivalents)
@item @file{niori-pdf-images.el} --- JPEG and PNG XObjects
@item @file{niori-pdf-flow.el} --- the typesetting engine
(@pxref{Flow Engine})
@item @file{niori-pdf-pdfa.el} --- PDF/A-2b archival output
(@pxref{PDF/A})
@end itemize

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

The package is built for MELPA; until published, install from a
checkout by adding the directory to @code{load-path} and requiring
@code{niori-pdf}.  The MELPA recipe is:

@lisp
(niori-pdf :fetcher github :repo "ianikeev/niori-pdf")
@end lisp

Running @code{make} byte-compiles the library and builds this manual:

@example
make            @r{compile + docs/niori-pdf.info}
make test       @r{run the ERT suite}
make dist       @r{release tarball}
@end example

The @code{info} target needs @command{makeinfo} (the Debian/Ubuntu
package is @code{texinfo}); the @code{compile} and @code{test} targets
need only Emacs.

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

@lisp
(require 'niori-pdf)

(niori-pdf-with-document (:title "Hello")
  (niori-pdf-with-page ()
    (niori-pdf-set-font "Helvetica-Bold" 24)
    (niori-pdf-draw-text 72 720 "Hello, PDF world")
    (niori-pdf-set-font "Helvetica" 12)
    (niori-pdf-draw-text 72 690 "Pure Emacs Lisp."))
  (niori-pdf-save niori-pdf--doc "/tmp/hello.pdf"))
@end lisp

Coordinates are PDF points (1/72 inch), origin at the bottom-left of
the page, y pointing up --- exactly like cl-pdf.  The default page is
A4 portrait (595.28 x 841.89 pt); pass @code{:width}/@code{:height} to
@code{niori-pdf-with-page} for anything else.

For reports with automatic layout, jump to @ref{Flow Engine}.

@menu
* Quick Start: Quick Start Flow.   A one-page report with the flow engine.
@end menu

@node Quick Start Flow
@section Quick start: the flow engine

The flow engine lays out whole documents for you --- justified
paragraphs, headings, tables, lists, images --- with automatic page
breaks, running headers and a table of contents:

@lisp
(require 'niori-pdf)

(niori-pdf-flow-generate
 (lambda ()
   (niori-pdf-with-flow-document
       (:title "Q4 report" :footer-fn #'niori-pdf-flow-page-footer)
     (niori-pdf-flow-heading "Q4 report" :level 1)
     (niori-pdf-flow-paragraph
      "Justified text with real font metrics, wrapped and paginated
automatically, hyphenated with TeX patterns."
      :align 'justified)
     (niori-pdf-flow-itemize '("first point" "second point"))
     (niori-pdf-flow-table
      (list :header ["Region" "Revenue"]
            :rows '(("North" "156") ("South" "134"))))
     (niori-pdf-flow-toc)))
 (niori-pdf-flow-save niori-pdf--doc "/tmp/report.pdf"))
@end lisp

When @code{niori-pdf-flow-toc} or cross-reference points are used,
@code{niori-pdf-flow-generate} runs the builder a second (at most
six) time so every page number in the output is correct.

@node Compression
@chapter Compression and zlib

This is the one area where Elisp needs an explanation, because GNU
Emacs bundles zlib @emph{for decompression only}: the API provides
@code{zlib-available-p} and @code{niori-pdf-inflate}/
@code{zlib-decompress-region}, but there is no built-in compression
primitive (there is no @code{zlib-insert}).

niori-pdf solves this in pure Elisp.  @code{niori-pdf-deflate}
implements DEFLATE (RFC 1951) with fixed Huffman codes and an LZ77
hash-chain matcher and wraps the result in an RFC 1950 zlib container
with an Adler-32 checksum.  The output is an ordinary
@code{/FlateDecode} stream that every PDF viewer understands.  The test
suite inflates it back with Emacs's own zlib as a round-trip check.

@defopt niori-pdf-compression
Stream compression strategy, one of:

@table @code
@item deflate
The pure Elisp implementation (default).  Always available, no external
dependencies, adequate compression for content streams and CMaps.
@item raw
Write streams uncompressed.  Useful for debugging.
@item gzip
Use the external program @code{niori-pdf-gzip-program} (default
@code{"gzip"}).  Its gzip container is stripped to the raw DEFLATE
payload, which is a valid @code{FlateDecode} stream.  Falls back to
@code{deflate} when the binary is missing.  This option breaks the
standalone-Emacs guarantee and is only worthwhile for very large
documents.
@end table
@end defopt

@defopt niori-pdf-deflate-min-size
Streams shorter than this many bytes (default 100) are written
uncompressed regardless of the strategy.
@end defopt

@defopt niori-pdf-deflate-max-chain
How many hash-chain candidates the LZ77 matcher examines per position
(default 16).  Higher values compress slightly better and run slower.
@end defopt

Note that Emacs's built-in decompression is still used where it
matters: PNG IDAT inflation (@code{zlib-decompress-region}) and the
test-suite round trips.  If Emacs was built without zlib, PNG images
with compressed IDAT raise an explicit error.

@node Documents
@chapter Documents and pages

@defmac niori-pdf-with-document (props) body@dots{}
Create a document context and run @var{body} inside it.  @var{props}
is a literal plist; supported keys: @code{:title :author :subject
:keywords :creator :producer :page-width :page-height :compression}.
The document is the value of the variable @code{niori-pdf--doc} during
@var{body} and the value of the whole form.
@end defmac

@defmac niori-pdf-with-page (props) body@dots{}
Create a page in the current document.  @var{props}: @code{:width
:height} (points).  All drawing forms inside operate on this page.
@end defmac

@defun niori-pdf-save doc file
Write a finished @var{doc}ument to @var{file} (classic xref table,
trailer with catalog and info dictionary).  Producer and creation date
are filled in automatically; info strings are written as UTF-16BE so
any Unicode title works.
@end defun

@node Text and Fonts
@chapter Text and Fonts

@defun niori-pdf-set-font name size
Select the current font.  @var{name} is one of:

@itemize
@item a base-14 name (@code{"Helvetica"}, @code{"Helvetica-Bold"},
@code{"Helvetica-Oblique"}, @code{"Helvetica-BoldOblique"},
@code{"Times-Roman"}, @code{"Times-Bold"}, @code{"Times-Italic"},
@code{"Times-BoldItalic"}, @code{"Courier"}, @code{"Courier-Bold"},
@code{"Courier-Oblique"}, @code{"Courier-BoldOblique"},
@code{"Symbol"}, @code{"ZapfDingbats"}); drawn unembedded with the
real Adobe AFM metrics,
@item a path to a @file{.ttf} TrueType, @file{.otf}
OpenType/CFF or @file{.ttc} TrueType collection file, which is
parsed, subsetted and embedded,
@item a path to a @file{.pfb} or @file{.pfa} Type 1 font file, which
is embedded whole with a @code{/FontFile} stream
(@pxref{Type 1 fonts}),
@item a plist @code{(:file PATH :index N)} selecting member @var{N}
(0-based) of a TrueType collection.
@end itemize
@end defun

@subheading Base-14 fonts
The base-14 fonts are drawn from the real Adobe AFM metrics (embedded in
@file{niori-pdf-metrics.el}, generated from the freely redistributable
core-14 AFM files).  Text is encoded with @code{WinAnsiEncoding}
(Latin-1 plus the Windows special characters such as the euro sign,
smart quotes and dashes).  Characters without a WinAnsi mapping are
drawn as @samp{?}.

@subheading TrueType, OpenType/CFF and TrueType collections
@code{niori-pdf-set-font} accepts @file{.ttf}, @file{.otf} and
@file{.ttc} paths.  TrueType and OpenType/CFF fonts are parsed (cmap
formats 0/4/6/12, head, hhea, maxp, hmtx, loca, glyf, name, post,
OS/2), a glyph subset covering exactly the characters you draw is
built (composite glyphs are closed transitively, original glyph ids
are retained) and the result is embedded as a @code{Type0} /
@code{CIDFontType2} (TrueType) or @code{CIDFontType0} (CFF) font with
@code{Identity-H} encoding and a @code{ToUnicode} CMap, so text
extraction keeps working.  Missing glyphs fall back to
@code{.notdef}.

A @file{.ttc} TrueType collection packs several fonts into one file;
niori-pdf reads the member selected by @var{index} (default 0).  Use
@code{niori-pdf-ttf-collection-fonts} to list the members and pass a
plist spec to choose one:

@lisp
(niori-pdf-ttf-collection-fonts "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/wqy/wqy-zenhei.ttc")
  @r{@result{} ((0 "WenQuanYiZenHei" "WenQuanYi Zen Hei")
           (1 "WenQuanYiZenHeiMono" "WenQuanYi Zen Hei Mono")
           (2 "WenQuanYiZenHeiSharp" "WenQuanYi Zen Hei Sharp"))}

;; draw with the second member of the collection:
(niori-pdf-set-font '(:file "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/wqy/wqy-zenhei.ttc"
                      :index 1)
                    12)
@end lisp

@defun niori-pdf-ttf-collection-fonts path
List the fonts of the TrueType collection at @var{path}.  Returns a
list of @code{(INDEX PS-NAME FAMILY)}.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-text-width string font &optional size
Width of @var{string} in points at @var{size}, using the same metrics
the viewer will use.  This is the measurement primitive of the flow
engine.  @var{font} may be a font object, a base-14 name, a font file
path or a @code{(:file :index)} collection spec.
@end defun

@node Type 1 fonts
@section Type 1 fonts (.pfb/.pfa)

Type 1 font programs are embedded whole --- no subsetting (Type 1
subsetting would mean rewriting the private dictionary; the fonts are
small anyway).  The parser handles both container formats: the binary
@file{.pfb} segment structure (@code{0x80 0x01} ASCII,
@code{0x80 0x02} binary, @code{0x80 0x03} EOF markers, with the
post-eexec hex trailer mapped to @code{/Length3}) and the ASCII
@file{.pfa} form (clear text up to the @code{eexec} line, then
hex-encoded binary).

No companion @file{.afm} file is needed: niori-pdf decrypts the
@code{eexec} section (key 55665) and reads the advance width of every
glyph directly from the @code{/CharStrings} charstrings (key 4330,
the first two operands of @code{hsbw}), so the PDF @code{/Widths}
array and the measurement metrics are derived from the font program
itself.  The result is a @code{/Subtype /Type1} font with
@code{/Encoding /WinAnsiEncoding} and a @code{/FontDescriptor}
carrying @code{/FontName}, @code{/FontBBox}, @code{/ItalicAngle},
@code{/CapHeight}, @code{/StemV} (from the private
@code{/StdVW}) and the standard flags (fixed-pitch, italic).

@lisp
;; URW Nimbus Roman, metric-compatible with Times-Roman:
(niori-pdf-set-font
 "/usr/share/fonts/X11/Type1/NimbusRoman-Regular.pfb" 10.5)
@end lisp

Caveat: the text you draw is mapped through WinAnsiEncoding glyph
names, so the font must contain the standard Latin glyph names
(all the URW Base-35 fonts do).

