niori-pdf-move-to / niori-pdf-line-to / niori-pdf-curve-to
niori-pdf-close-path / niori-pdf-rectangle / niori-pdf-circle
niori-pdf-ellipse / niori-pdf-arc / niori-pdf-polyline
niori-pdf-stroke / niori-pdf-close-stroke / niori-pdf-fill
niori-pdf-fill-stroke / niori-pdf-clip
niori-pdf-set-line-width / niori-pdf-set-dash-pattern
niori-pdf-set-rgb-fill / niori-pdf-set-rgb-stroke
niori-pdf-set-gray-fill / niori-pdf-set-gray-stroke
niori-pdf-set-cmyk-fill / niori-pdf-set-cmyk-stroke
niori-pdf-set-color-fill / niori-pdf-set-color-stroke  @r{named colors}
niori-pdf-set-fill-alpha / niori-pdf-set-stroke-alpha   @r{transparency}
niori-pdf-translate / niori-pdf-rotate / niori-pdf-scale
niori-pdf-skew / niori-pdf-transform
niori-pdf-with-saved-state  @r{macro: q/Q around the body}
niori-pdf-draw-text / niori-pdf-draw-text-at
niori-pdf-draw-centered-text / niori-pdf-draw-right-text
niori-pdf-add-uri-link / niori-pdf-add-link
@end example

@node Images
@chapter Images

@defun niori-pdf-image-from-file spec
Load an image from a file path or a plist @code{(:type jpeg|png :data
BYTES)}.  JPEG files are embedded as @code{DCTDecode} XObjects
(dimensions and colorspace parsed from the markers; grayscale, RGB and
Adobe-inverted CMYK are supported).  PNG files are fully decoded in
Elisp: chunk parsing, zlib inflation, row un-filtering (None, Sub, Up,
Average, Paeth), color types 0/2/3/4/6, bit depths 1/2/4/8 and 16
(downsampled to 8).  Palette images with a @code{tRNS} chunk and
RGBA/gray-alpha images get an alpha @code{/SMask}.  Interlaced PNGs are
rejected.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-draw-image image x y &optional dx dy
Draw an image object with its lower-left corner at (@var{x}, @var{y}),
scaled to @var{dx} x @var{dy} points (default: one point per pixel).
@end defun

@node Flow Engine
@chapter The Flow Engine

The flow engine is the Elisp counterpart of cl-typesetting: you declare
blocks, it wraps lines, breaks pages and keeps headings with their
content.  All block functions operate on the dynamic flow context
established by @code{niori-pdf-with-flow-document}.

@defmac niori-pdf-with-flow-document (props) body@dots{}
Establish a flow context.  @var{props}: @code{:width :height :margins}
(list @code{(left top right bottom)}, default @code{(54 56 54 56)})
@code{:header-fn :footer-fn} (functions of @code{(flow page-number)})
@code{:default-font :default-size} @code{:language :hyphenate :vjustify}
@code{:pdfa} (enable PDF/A-2b archival output, @pxref{PDF/A}) and the
document info keys.
@end defmac

@defun niori-pdf-flow-generate builder
Run @var{builder} (a thunk that calls the block functions) and return
the document.  The builder is re-run until the layout is stable: the
table-of-contents entries, the reference points recorded with
@code{niori-pdf-flow-mark-ref-point} and the per-page footnote block
heights must stop changing between passes (at most six passes), so
the TOC, every cross-reference and the footnotes show correct
results.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-save doc file
Write the document (same as @code{niori-pdf-save}).
@end defun

@subheading Blocks

@defun niori-pdf-flow-paragraph content &rest keys
@var{content} is a string or a list of runs; a run is a string or a
@code{(TEXT &key font size color superscript subscript underline
strike background)} form.  @code{:superscript}/@code{:subscript} set
the run at 66% of the base size with the appropriate baseline
offset, @code{:underline} and @code{:strike} draw rules under and
through the run, and @code{:background} fills an RGB rectangle
behind it.  Paragraph keys: @code{:align
left|center|right|justified}, @code{:font :size :leading :color
:indent :space-before :space-after}.  Paragraphs are broken with the Knuth-Plass algorithm over the
box/glue/penalty model (three TeX-style passes with pretolerance,
tolerance and emergency stretch); lines are set with TJ
adjustments.  With @code{:align justified} the last line keeps its
alignment.  Hyphenation uses TeX patterns via @code{:hyphenate}
(default t) and @code{:language} (@code{american british german
french spanish italian portuguese esperanto ukrainian russian}; the
Portuguese set covers both European and Brazilian Portuguese).  Words
that contain an explicit hyphen (compound
identifiers such as @code{niori-pdf-curve-to}) follow the TeX rule:
pattern hyphenation is disabled for them and the break opportunities
sit after their existing hyphens, with no second hyphen glyph at the
line end.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-heading text &key level font size color space-before space-after toc
Styled heading (levels 1..4 with default styles).  Headings reserve
space for the following block (keep-with-next) and, unless @code{toc}
is nil, record an entry for the table of contents.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-table spec
Grid table.  @var{spec} keys: @code{:cols} (widths or @code{:auto}),
@code{:header}, @code{:rows}, @code{:padding}, @code{:header-bg
:header-fg}, @code{:borders} (RGB list or nil), @code{:font :size
:align}.  A cell is a string or a plist @code{(:text :font :size
:color :align :background :colspan :rowspan :valign)}: @code{:colspan}
and @code{:rowspan} span the grid (@code{:valign} is
@code{top}, @code{center} or @code{bottom} inside the spanned
rectangle).  A row is a list of cells --- or a plist @code{(:cells
:keep-with-next :background)}: @code{:keep-with-next} moves the row
to the next page together with its successor and @code{:background}
fills the whole row.  Cell text wraps; row heights are computed from
the wrapped cells (a rowspan cell contributes to the sum of its
covered rows); tables split across pages, repeat the header row, and
never break inside a span.  Columns whose minimum word width exceeds
their width are widened, funded by the surplus columns.
@code{:space-after} adds vertical space between the closing rule and
the next block (default 0).
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-image path &key width max-height align caption
Image block, scaled to @var{width} (fitting @var{max-height}), with an
optional centered caption.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-box builder &key background border padding width align
Draw a box around the blocks produced by @var{builder}; useful for KPI
cards and callouts.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-columns builders &key widths gap row-height
Lay out several builders side by side (KPI rows); the row is as tall as
the tallest column.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-rule &key thickness color indent space-before space-after
Horizontal rule.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-spacer h
@defunx niori-pdf-flow-vglue &optional stretch
@defunx niori-pdf-flow-vfill
@defunx niori-pdf-flow-page-break
Fixed vertical space; stretchable vertical glue (like TeX's
@code{\vskip} with @var{stretch} units of stretch); infinitely
stretchable glue (@code{\vfill}); forced page break.  When a page
ends, the leftover space above the bottom margin is distributed over
its vertical glues proportionally to their stretch --- blocks after a
glue are shifted down with a PDF @code{cm} transform, so text, rules
and images all move together.  Give the document @code{:vjustify t}
to treat every block boundary as a weak glue (vertical
justification), as in cl-typesetting's filled vboxes.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-itemize items &key bullet bullet-color indent gap font size color align space-between space-before space-after hyphenate language
Bulleted list.  @var{items} are strings or run lists; each item is a
paragraph with a hanging indent and the @var{bullet} drawn left of
its first line.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-enumerate items &key start format marker-color indent gap font size color align space-between space-before space-after hyphenate language
Numbered list; markers are formatted with @var{format} (default
@code{"%d."}) from @var{start} (default 1) and right-aligned in a
common column.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-float builder &key width side gap
Float the blocks produced by @var{builder} (a thunk) to @var{side}
(@code{left}, default, or @code{right}), @var{width} points wide.
Paragraphs and rules on the same page wrap around the float; the
flow cursor stays at the float's top, so content fills the space
beside it.  Floats do not survive a page break.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-toc
Table of contents.  Place it early in the document (typically after a
title block, before a @code{niori-pdf-flow-page-break}).  Entries are
collected from the headings; @code{niori-pdf-flow-generate} re-runs the
builder so page numbers are correct.
@end defun

@subheading Reference points (cross references)

@defun niori-pdf-flow-mark-ref-point id &optional data
Record a reference point @var{id} at the current position (with
arbitrary @var{data}).  Collected across the multi-pass layout.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-ref-page-number id &optional default
@defunx niori-pdf-flow-ref-data id
@defunx niori-pdf-flow-ref id
Page number / data / whole entry of reference point @var{id} from
the previous pass (nil on the first pass) --- use them inside your
own content to print ``see chapter N''.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-flow-put-ref-page-number id &key font size color align unresolved
Typeset the page number of @var{id}, right-aligned by default; on
the first pass @var{unresolved} (an em dash) is printed instead and
the multi-pass loop re-runs the builder until the real number
appears.
@end defun

@subheading Footnotes

@defun niori-pdf-flow-footnote text &key marker font size leading color
Register @var{text} as a footnote for the current page and return its
@var{marker}.  The note is set in a block at the bottom of the page,
below a short LaTeX-style separator rule (@code{niori-pdf-flow-footnote-rule-width}
of the text measure; color @code{niori-pdf-flow-footnote-rule-color}),
when the page is flushed.  @var{marker} defaults to the note's number
on the page (@samp{1}, @samp{2}, @dots{}); embed it in the citing
paragraph as a superscript run:

@example
(let ((fn (niori-pdf-flow-footnote "Studio notes.")))
  (niori-pdf-flow-paragraph
   (list "Text cites a source" (list fn :superscript t) ".") ...))
@end example

@var{font}, @var{size} (default 8), @var{leading} (default 1.24) and
@var{color} style the note text.  Pages that carry footnotes are
shortened by their note block's height on the next layout pass of
@code{niori-pdf-flow-generate}, so the notes always fit between the
text and the bottom margin.  Register the note adjacent to the
paragraph that cites it: a note belongs to the page that is current
when @code{niori-pdf-flow-footnote} runs.
@end defun

@subheading Page furniture

Two ready-made template functions can be passed as
@code{:header-fn}/@code{:footer-fn}:
@code{niori-pdf-flow-page-footer} draws a centered
@samp{- @var{n} -} page number; @code{niori-pdf-flow-page-header} draws
the document title left and the page number right in the top margin.
Custom functions receive the flow context and the page number and may
use the ordinary content-level operators.

@node Outlines
@chapter Outlines (Bookmarks)

@code{niori-pdf-outlines.el} builds the PDF outline tree (the bookmark
panel).  Flow headings record outline entries automatically; for raw
documents add entries with @code{niori-pdf-outlines-add}.

@defun niori-pdf-outlines-add doc title page &key level y
Add an outline entry @var{title} pointing to @var{page} (1-based) of
@var{doc}.  @var{level} (default 1) nests under the previous entry of
the nearest shallower level; @var{y} is the vertical destination in PDF
coordinates (nil = page top).
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-outlines-count doc
Number of accumulated entries.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-outlines-clear doc
Forget all entries of @var{doc}.
@end defun

The tree is written during @code{niori-pdf-save}; the catalog receives
@code{/Outlines} and @code{/PageMode /UseOutlines}.  A document with
outline entries only (no flow TOC) works fine as well.

@node AcroForms
@chapter AcroForms (Interactive Forms)

@code{niori-pdf-acroforms.el} adds interactive form fields (PDF
32000-1 chapter 12.7).  Call @code{niori-pdf-acroforms-enable} once
inside the document, then create fields with page coordinates (origin
bottom-left, like @code{niori-pdf-add-link}).  Every field gets a
generated appearance stream, so the form renders correctly even in
viewers that ignore @code{NeedAppearances}.

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-enable doc &optional need-appearances
Enable form collection for @var{doc}.  With @var{need-appearances} the
catalog gets @code{/NeedAppearances true} (by default niori-pdf
provides its own appearance streams and omits the flag).
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-text-field doc name x y w h &key value font font-size tooltip multiline password read-only align max-len bg bc
Text field.  @var{align} is 0 (left), 1 (center) or 2 (right).
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-checkbox doc name x y w h &key checked read-only tooltip bg bc mark
Check box with a path-drawn check mark appearance.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-choice doc name x y w h options &key value editable tooltip read-only bg bc mark
Dropdown (combo) choice; @var{options} is a list of strings.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-radio-group doc name buttons &key value read-only tooltip bg bc mark
Radio group; @var{buttons} is a list of @code{(x y size
export-string)} tuples on the current page.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-push-button doc name x y w h &key caption tooltip uri font font-size read-only bg bc fg
Push button; with @var{uri} it triggers a URI action.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-acroforms-set-value doc name value
Update a field's value (and appearance) after creation: strings for
text and choice fields, booleans for check boxes, export strings for
radio groups.
@end defun

@node Barcodes
@chapter Barcodes and QR codes

@code{niori-pdf-barcodes.el} ports cl-pdf's @file{bar-codes.lisp}
(Code 128 by Lars Rustemeier) and adds Code 39 and EAN-13, plus the
module-matrix drawer shared with the QR encoder
(@code{niori-pdf-qrcode.el}).  All barcodes are drawn with path
primitives, so no fonts are embedded for the bars themselves.

@defun niori-pdf-barcode-code128 string x y &key height width font font-size start-stop-factor show-string color
Code 128 with automatic charset selection (C for even-length
all-digit strings, B otherwise).  The bars rise upward from the
baseline @var{y}; the human readable text is drawn below it.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-barcode-code39 string x y &key height width narrow show-text font font-size color
Code 3 of 9 (@code{0-9 A-Z - . space $ / + %}).
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-barcode-ean13 string x y &key height width font font-size color
EAN-13; accepts 12 or 13 digits (the check digit is computed for 12).
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-barcode-qr matrix x y &key point-size color
Draw a module @var{matrix} (list of lists of 0/1) as filled squares,
row 0 at the anchor with the y axis running downward (the port of
cl-pdf's @code{draw-qr-code}).
@end defun

@node QRCode
@chapter QR codes

@code{niori-pdf-qrcode.el} is a full QR Code model 2 encoder
(ISO/IEC 18004) written for niori-pdf - cl-pdf only draws a
pre-computed matrix.  Features: byte mode (UTF-8, no ECI segment),
versions 1-10, all four error correction levels, Reed-Solomon error
correction over GF(256) with the proper block structure and
interleaving, all eight mask patterns scored with the ISO penalty
rules N1-N4, BCH(15,5) format information and BCH(18,6) version
information for versions 7-10.  Every matrix emitted by the test
suite round-trips through an independent QR decoder.

@defun niori-pdf-qr-encode string &key ecl version
Return @code{(MATRIX . VERSION)}; @var{matrix} is a list of N lists
of N integers (1 = dark, 0 = light), row 0 at the top.  @var{ecl} is
the symbol @code{L}, @code{M}, @code{Q} or @code{H} (default
@code{M}); @var{version} forces a version 1-10, otherwise the
smallest one that fits is used.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-barcode-qr-string string x y &rest keys
Encode @var{string} and draw the symbol with its top-left module
corner at (@var{x}, @var{y}).  Keys: @code{:ecl :version
:point-size :color}.  Returns @code{(WIDTH HEIGHT VERSION)}.
@end defun

@node Charts
@chapter Charts

@code{niori-pdf-charts.el} ports the chart helpers of cl-pdf's
@file{chart.lisp}: pie charts and axis bar charts drawn as vector
paths with legend and value labels.

@defun niori-pdf-pie-chart data x y radius &rest keys
@var{data} is a list of @code{(LABEL VALUE COLOR)}; keys:
@code{:title :show-values :label-font :label-size}.
@end defun

@defun niori-pdf-bar-chart data x y width height &rest keys
@var{data} as above; keys: @code{:title :y-axis-labels :grid-color
:bar-gap :show-values :label-font :label-size}.
@end defun

@node Glyph Outlines
@chapter Glyph Outlines

@code{niori-pdf-glyphs.el} extracts the outline contours of a glyph
from a TrueType (@file{glyf}) or CFF/OpenType (@file{CharStrings})
font and draws them with path primitives.  This is the port of
cl-pdf's glyph rendering; it is handy for watermark-style decorative
lettering at any size.

@defun niori-pdf-draw-glyph-outline font-file char x y size &key fill stroke line-width
Draw the outline of @var{char} from @var{font-file} (a .ttf or .otf
path) with its baseline at (@var{x}, @var{y}) scaled to @var{size}
points.  @var{fill}/@var{stroke} are RGB lists or nil.
@end defun

@node PDF/A
@chapter PDF/A-2b archival output

PDF/A (ISO 19005) is the archival flavor of PDF: everything needed to
render the file is embedded, so the document displays identically
decades from now.  niori-pdf implements the pragmatic flavor,
@strong{PDF/A-2b} (level B, based on PDF 1.7): transparency, JPEG
images and URI/GoTo link actions stay legal, and no structure tagging
is required.  PDF/A-1 is deliberately not supported (it forbids
transparency), nor is PDF/A-3 (it adds embedded files, which archival
reports rarely need).

@defun niori-pdf-pdfa-enable doc &optional level
Enable PDF/A-2b output for @var{doc}.  @var{level} must be 2 (the
default).  Registers the metadata/output-intent catalog extension and
the annotation save hook; @code{niori-pdf-save} then writes a
@code{%PDF-1.7} header and an @code{/ID} trailer.  Returns
@var{doc}.  In flow documents, simply pass @code{:pdfa t} to
@code{niori-pdf-with-flow-document} instead of calling this
function.
@end defun

What changes when PDF/A is enabled:

@itemize
@item @code{/Metadata} --- the document catalog gets an uncompressed
XMP packet with @code{pdfaid:part}/@code{pdfaid:conformance} and the
dublin-core properties (title, creator, subject, keywords), mirroring
the Info dictionary byte for byte as ISO 19005-2 6.6.2 requires.
@item @code{/OutputIntents} --- a @code{GTS_PDFA1} OutputIntent with
an embedded ICC profile as @code{/DestOutputProfile}, so DeviceRGB and
DeviceGray colors are valid.  The profile is generated in pure Elisp:
a minimal, deterministic ICC v2 matrix/TRC profile (sRGB primaries,
gamma 2.2 TRCs, D50 media white point; about 600 bytes).  It is a
structurally valid ICC profile --- verified against lcms2 --- but it
describes a gamma-2.2 approximation of sRGB rather than the exact
sRGB parametric curve.
@item @code{/ID} --- the trailer carries an MD5-based /ID array (also
written for plain documents; harmless and useful).
@item Link annotations get the Print flag (@code{/F 4}, ISO 19005-2
6.8).
@item Fonts --- every font must be embedded (ISO 19005 forbids
unembedded fonts, without exception for Symbol/ZapfDingbats).  The
base-14 names are therefore rejected unless
@code{niori-pdf-pdfa-base14-substitutions} maps them to a font file;
@code{.ttf}/@code{.otf}/@code{.ttc}/@code{.pfb}/@code{.pfa} fonts
embed as usual.
@item Color --- DeviceCMYK is rejected (the built-in OutputIntent is
sRGB); use RGB or grayscale.
@end itemize

@defopt niori-pdf-pdfa-base14-substitutions
Alist mapping base-14 font names to embedded font specs used in PDF/A
documents, e.g.:

@lisp
(setq niori-pdf-pdfa-base14-substitutions
      '(("Helvetica" . "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans.ttf")
        ("Helvetica-Bold"
         . "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans-Bold.ttf")
        ("Symbol" . "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans.ttf")))
@end lisp

The replacement can be any font spec accepted by
@code{niori-pdf-set-font}.  Since Symbol/ZapfDingbats text is drawn
from Unicode strings through the font's own encoding map, mapping them
to a TrueType font with the right codepoints (DejaVu Sans covers the dingbats and Greek used in the examples) preserves the glyph
appearance without the unembedded base-14 fonts.
@end defopt

